.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm librelic
library evaluates Datalog programs with stratified negation.
A context,
.Vt relic_t ,
holds a program, the values it mentions and the relations derived from it.
Contexts share nothing, so a program may use as many as it likes and use
distinct ones from distinct threads, but one context is not thread-safe.
.Pp
A program is loaded as text with
.Fn relic_load_string ,
.Fn relic_load_file
or
.Fn relic_load_path ,
and grown with facts built through the interface with
.Fn relic_fact_add
and facts read from csv and tsv tables with
.Fn relic_import_file
and
.Fn relic_import_path .
Loads, facts and imports append to one program in the order they are made.
.Fn relic_load_string
takes NUL-terminated text,
.Fn relic_load_file
reads
.Fa stream
to its end and leaves it open, and
.Fn relic_load_path
opens
.Fa path ,
reads it and closes it; a
.Dv NULL
text, stream or path is refused.
.Fa source_name
names the text in diagnostics, is copied, and is
.Qq <input>
when
.Dv NULL .
A load or an import is all or nothing.
A load, an import, or a call to
.Fn relic_fact_add ,
whose facts would take the relations past
.Fa max_tuples
or
.Fa max_memory
is refused with nothing added, and is parsed no further than the first fact
shown not to fit; when the facts are what do not fit, the error names the
first predicate, in the order the text names them, that the facts read
would add to:
.Dq limit exceeded: tuples=4 while loading e/1 .
Queries are prepared from text, such as
.Qq path(a, X)?\& ,
with
.Fn relic_query_prepare ,
or taken from the program with
.Fn relic_query_loaded ,
and answered with
.Fn relic_query_run
or
.Fn relic_query_collect .
.Ss Contexts
.Fn relic_create
creates a context with every limit at its default, and
.Fn relic_create_config
one with the limits of
.Fa config ,
or the defaults when it is
.Dv NULL
or zeroed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct relic_config {
	size_t		max_memory;
	size_t		max_tuples;
	size_t		max_magic_preds;
	size_t		max_magic_rules;
	unsigned int	flags;
	size_t		threads;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa max_tuples
bounds the tuples held across every relation, the program's facts included,
and
.Fa max_memory
the bytes of relation and index storage, the program's facts included,
together with the table of the integers the program states and evaluation
computes, the symbol pool, which holds every symbol and string the program
states and every string evaluation computes, the names of the predicates
the program and the rewrites of its queries name and of the sources each
was first read from, the buffers an
evaluation computes strings in, the plans of an evaluation and the rewrite
of a query; zero leaves either unbounded.
.Fa max_memory
does not bound all the memory the library uses: the text being loaded, the
program's rules and queries, the field of a table being imported, a
prepared query and the entry and index slot the context keeps beside each
predicate's name are sized by what the caller wrote, and growing an array
holds the old one until the new one is filled.
Nothing those tables hold is released before the context is destroyed:
every distinct integer, symbol and string a load, a fact, the constants of
a query or an evaluation interns, and every predicate a query or the
rewrite of one names, stays for the life of the context, the values and the
names counting against
.Fa max_memory .
A context that goes on answering queries over new constants therefore grows
until it reaches the limit, and a caller serving such queries for as long as
it runs bounds that growth by loading the program into a new context and
destroying the old one.
No limit bounds the time an evaluation takes: a join over a few small
relations whose every result is a duplicate stays inside both however long
it runs, so a caller evaluating a program it does not trust must bound the
time itself.
.Fa max_magic_preds
and
.Fa max_magic_rules
bound the predicates and rules the Magic Sets rewrite of one query
generates.
With both zero, the rewrite has budgets in proportion to the program, on
the predicates it generates and the literals of the rules it drafts, a
literal with an expression counting one more per node of it, which bound
the rules it keeps as well, and a rewrite outgrowing one is abandoned with
a warning and the query answered from the whole program, which never
answers differently but may meet an undefined arithmetic result, a string
too long or an invalid pattern the rewrite would not have; setting
either lifts every budget, and a limit left zero beside it bounds nothing.
The rewrite searches for what to generate before it generates anything,
and the search is held to the same limits and budgets; it can count
predicates and rules the finished rewrite drops, so a limit may refuse a
query whose rewrite would have fit.
.Dv SIZE_MAX
sets no bound on anything.
.Pp
.Fa threads
is how many threads an evaluation may share a round's work among, the
calling thread included; zero and one both mean the calling thread alone,
which is the default, and more than
.Dv RELIC_THREADS_MAX ,
256, is refused.
The threads are the context's own: they are started the first time an
evaluation shares a round, run only inside an operation the caller started,
call none of the caller's callbacks, and are joined when the context is
destroyed.
They block every signal but those a fault in them raises, so a signal sent
to the process is taken by one of the caller's threads.
The calling thread cannot be cancelled while it waits for them.
Their stacks and the working state each keeps for running plans are not
counted by
.Fa max_memory .
A child of
.Xr fork 2
has none of its parent's threads.
Forked between operations, it may go on using a context its parent created,
whose rounds then run on the calling thread alone, and destroying the
context there joins nothing; this relies on the C library's allocator
working in a child of a process with threads, which POSIX does not promise
but the systems relic runs on provide.
A context forked while one of its operations runs is refused in the child
as it would be in the parent until that operation returns, which in the
child it never does.
A round is shared only when it holds enough work to repay the threads'
coordination, and the answers, the relations, every diagnostic and every
counter but
.Fa parallel_rounds
are the same whatever the number of threads.
A thread that cannot be started is not an error: the evaluation uses those
that could be.
A library built without threads accepts the field and runs every round on
the calling thread.
.Pp
.Fa flags
holds switches, zero by default.
.Dv RELIC_F_NAIVE ,
.Dv RELIC_F_NO_INDEX ,
.Dv RELIC_F_NO_REORDER ,
.Dv RELIC_F_NO_MAGIC ,
.Dv RELIC_F_NO_STREAM
and
.Dv RELIC_F_PARALLEL_ALWAYS
select alternative evaluation strategies for testing, and
.Dv RELIC_F_NOTE_MIXED
asks for a diagnostic:
.Bl -tag -width RELIC_F_PARALLEL_ALWAYS
.It Dv RELIC_F_NAIVE
evaluate every recursive component by the naive fixed point rather than the
semi-naive one;
.It Dv RELIC_F_NO_INDEX
build no secondary index, scanning instead;
.It Dv RELIC_F_NO_REORDER
join positive literals in the order they are written, but for the one
reading what the previous round derived, which goes first;
.It Dv RELIC_F_NO_MAGIC
answer every query from the whole program rather than through its Magic
Sets rewrite;
.It Dv RELIC_F_NOTE_MIXED
report, as a note pointing at it, the first comparison or aggregate in each
rule that had no value: a comparison that ordered values of different types,
applied an arithmetic operator to a symbol or a string, or called a string
function with an argument of a type it does not take, which is false, or
an aggregate whose rows were not all integers for a sum, or not all of
one type for a minimum or a maximum; a
rule of the program is reported at most once for the life of the context,
the rules a Magic Sets rewrite generates from it counting as that rule, and
a query at most once each time it is answered;
.It Dv RELIC_F_NO_STREAM
gather every query's answer whole before delivering its first row, even a
query whose plan derives each row once and could deliver its rows as it
derives them; the rows and their order are the same;
.It Dv RELIC_F_PARALLEL_ALWAYS
defer the insertions of every round of evaluation that reads none of the
relations it derives except as they stood when it began, which is every
round but a pass of the naive fixed point, to the round's end, cutting the
scan each rule starts from into pieces, as a round run on several threads
is run; the relations and every count of work are the same.
.El
.Pp
No switch changes an answer, though two strategies may hold different
amounts while they compute one, and so one may meet a limit the other does
not.
A bit no switch defines is ignored.
.Pp
Each context keys its hash tables with a seed drawn from
.Xr getentropy 2
when it is created, so that a program cannot be written to crowd its keys
into one part of a table and make every lookup slow.
No answer, identifier, limit or line of explain output depends on the seed.
The seed defends a context against input written before it was created.
A party that feeds one long-lived context input over time, and can time
the requests it makes, may in principle learn which of its keys share part
of a table and so make the context slow; a context used that way trusts
its input not to do so.
.Pp
.Fn relic_destroy
releases a context and everything it owns, and accepts
.Dv NULL .
.Fn relic_version
returns the version of the library the caller is linked against, as
.Qq major.minor.patch ,
in a static string.
The header defines the version it belongs to as
.Dv RELIC_VERSION_MAJOR ,
.Dv RELIC_VERSION_MINOR ,
.Dv RELIC_VERSION_PATCH ,
the string
.Dv RELIC_VERSION_STRING ,
and
.Dv RELIC_VERSION_NUMBER ,
which is major \(mu 10000 + minor \(mu 100 + patch, so that a caller can
require a version in one comparison; a shared library upgraded since the
caller was compiled can make the two differ.
.Ss Values
A value is a symbol, a string or an integer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct relic_value {
	enum relic_type	type;	/* RELIC_SYMBOL, RELIC_STRING, RELIC_INTEGER */
	union {
		uint32_t	id;
		int64_t		integer;
	} v;
};
.Ed
.Pp
An integer is written into
.Fa v.integer .
.Fn relic_value_symbol
and
.Fn relic_value_string
intern
.Fa length
bytes and fill
.Fa value
with their identifier, which is valid for the life of the context.
A symbol must be a lowercase ASCII letter followed by ASCII letters, digits
and underscores, as the language writes one;
.Fn relic_value_symbol
refuses any other bytes, a NUL among them, which only
.Fn relic_value_string
takes.
A context holds at most 2^30 \- 1 distinct byte strings, which symbols and
strings share, and at most 2^30 \- 1 distinct integers; a byte string is
at most 2^32 \- 2 bytes long.
Interning past a limit, or past
.Fa max_memory ,
fails with the reason reported.
.Fn relic_string
returns the bytes of a symbol or string identifier, NUL-terminated, and
stores their length in
.Fa *lenp
when it is not
.Dv NULL ;
it returns
.Dv NULL
for an identifier the context never assigned, a collected result's among
them.
A value carries no owner, only its identifier: another context's identifier
that this one has assigned too reads as this context's value, so a value is
passed only to the context or result that made it.
The bytes of a value the program holds, one it states or one a caller
built, stay valid until the context is destroyed; those of a value a query
computed are valid while the row callback receiving it runs.
.Fn relic_value_compare
orders two values: every integer before every symbol before every string,
then numerically or bytewise.
A value the context does not hold compares equal to everything, and is
reported as an error unless caller code the library is running made the
call.
.Ss Facts
.Fn relic_fact_add
adds the fact
.Fa predicate Ns ( Fa values Ns [0], ...)
to the program exactly as loading its text would.
.Fa predicate
must be a symbol other than
.Ql not ,
.Fa arity
at most 32, and
.Fa values
may be
.Dv NULL
only when
.Fa arity
is zero.
Each value must be an integer or an identifier the context assigned, so a
value read out of an answer is built again from its bytes, and a
value typed
.Dv RELIC_SYMBOL
must hold a symbol's bytes.
A predicate is its name and its arity together, so a fact of a new arity
starts a new predicate.
The fact is stored with the program's other facts before the call returns,
or refused with nothing added.
Adding a fact the program does not hold changes the program, so everything
derived before it is discarded and the next evaluation derives it again;
adding one it holds changes nothing.
.Ss Imports
.Fn relic_import_file
and
.Fn relic_import_path
add the facts a table holds, one per record, as though the program had
stated them.
The import is described by a
.Vt relic_import_t :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct relic_import {
	const char		*predicate;
	const enum relic_column	*columns;
	size_t			 column_count;
	enum relic_format	 format;
	unsigned int		 flags;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa format
is
.Dv RELIC_FORMAT_CSV
or
.Dv RELIC_FORMAT_TSV ;
both enumerations start at 1, so a zeroed description names no format and
no column.
The tables are
each read as the exact inverse of the
.Xr relic 1
output format of the same name.
In csv, fields are separated by commas, and a field that begins with a
double quote is quoted: it holds commas and line ends as data, writes a
quote as two, and ends at its closing quote.
In tsv, fields are separated by tabs, and a backslash begins one of the
escapes
.Ql \e\e ,
.Ql \et ,
.Ql \en
and
.Ql \er ,
and no other.
In both, a record ends at a line feed, or a carriage return and a line
feed, outside quotes; the last needs no line end; and an empty line is a
record of one empty field.
A carriage return anywhere else outside quotes is an error, as is, in csv,
a quote inside an unquoted field or anything but a comma or a line end
after a closing quote.
Bytes are data: no encoding is assumed and no space is trimmed.
.Pp
Each of the
.Fa column_count
entries of
.Fa columns
types the field in its position:
.Bl -tag -width RELIC_COLUMN_INTEGER
.It Dv RELIC_COLUMN_SYMBOL
a symbol, as
.Fn relic_value_symbol
accepts one;
.It Dv RELIC_COLUMN_STRING
a string of the field's bytes;
.It Dv RELIC_COLUMN_INTEGER
an integer as the language writes one, an optional minus and decimal
digits, within
.Vt int64_t ;
.It Dv RELIC_COLUMN_SKIP
nothing: the field is read and is not part of the fact.
.El
.Pp
A field is typed after its format has decoded it, so a quoted
.Ql \&"7"
in a csv integer column is 7.
Nothing is inferred: a field its column refuses is an error.
The fact's arity is the number of columns not skipped, at most 32, and
every record must have a field per column, skipped ones included.
With
.Dv RELIC_IMPORT_HEADER
in
.Fa flags ,
the first record is a header: it must be there and have a field per column,
and is not imported.
.Pp
An import stops at its first mistake, which it reports at its line and
column, and adds nothing: a table that cannot be read, a malformed record, a
field its column refuses and a limit the facts do not fit all fail it.
Facts already held are held once.
.Fn relic_import_path
opens
.Fa path
and names diagnostics by it;
.Fn relic_import_file
reads
.Fa stream
and names them
.Fa source_name ,
or
.Qq <input>
when it is
.Dv NULL .
The description is refused, with nothing read, when it is
.Dv NULL ,
names no valid predicate, has no columns or a column that is not a
.Vt relic_column ,
keeps more than 32 columns, names no format, or sets a flag the library
does not define; and so is an import given a
.Dv NULL
.Fa stream
or
.Fa path .
The description is borrowed for the call.
.Ss Evaluation
.Fn relic_check
validates the program: rule safety, stratification, and warnings about
variables named once and predicates nothing defines.
It checks only what was loaded since the previous check, so a second check
with nothing loaded between repeats none of the first's messages, and once
the program has held a rejected rule it fails for good, since loading more
cannot remove one; a failing check whose mistakes were reported before
reports only that the program cannot be used.
A variable an aggregate shares with the rest of its rule must be bound
both inside the aggregate's body and outside it, and a program recursive
through an aggregate is rejected as one recursive through negation is.
.Fn relic_materialize
evaluates the whole program.
Neither is needed before a query, which checks and evaluates what it needs.
An evaluation fails, and the next starts again from the facts, when a limit
refuses what it would hold, when memory runs out, when an arithmetic
expression has no defined result: one outside the 64-bit integers, a
division or remainder by zero, or the remainder of the least integer by
\-1, when a string function would compute a
string longer than a string value can be, or when
.Ql match
is given a pattern that is not a regular expression; a sum whose total is
outside the 64-bit integers fails it in the same way.
.Pp
.Fn relic_interrupt
asks the operation in progress on
.Fa ctx
to stop.
An evaluation, by
.Fn relic_materialize ,
.Fn relic_query_run
or
.Fn relic_query_collect ,
looks for the request every few thousand steps of a join and before each
row of an answer computed whole, and fails on finding it, reporting
.Qq interrupted
and what it was deriving or delivering; the next evaluation starts again
from the facts.
Every other operation runs to its end.
.Fn relic_interrupt
only records the request: it may be called from a signal handler, from
another thread, or from a callback of the operation, while the caller keeps
the context alive.
A library built by a compiler without C11 atomics may be interrupted from a
signal handler or a callback but not from another thread.
An operation forgets any request made before it began, so an interrupt
with no operation in progress stops nothing.
It accepts
.Dv NULL .
.Ss Queries
.Fn relic_query_run
answers
.Fa query
and passes the answer to
.Fa callback
one row at a time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef int (*relic_row_cb)(void *callback_ctx,
    const relic_value_t *values, size_t count);
.Ed
.Pp
A row has one value per variable the query names, in the order they first
occur, but for
.Ql _
and the variables local to an aggregate's braces; a query with no variables
has one row of no values when it holds and none when it does not.
The values are borrowed for the call, and so are the bytes
.Fn relic_string
reads for a symbol or string among them: a callback that keeps a row
copies the bytes, or the caller collects the answer instead.
Rows are distinct, in an order the language leaves unspecified but that is
the same on every run over the same input.
The callback returns zero for the next row and nonzero to stop, and
.Fn relic_query_run
then returns 0.
No row is delivered until everything that can fail has succeeded, so a query
that fails delivers no rows, but for one interrupted by
.Fn relic_interrupt
after its first row: it fails all the same, and the rows it delivered are a
prefix of its answer.
In a context where
.Fa max_tuples
and
.Fa max_memory
each set no limit, being zero or
.Dv SIZE_MAX ,
a query delivers its rows as it derives them, once every relation it reads
is complete, when the plan of the rule it runs, its own or its rewrite's,
derives each row once and computes nothing: when it names every variable a
relational literal of its body binds, matches no argument of a positive
atom with
.Ql _ ,
evaluates no arithmetic operator, calls no function, and holds no
aggregate, any of which may fail.
Any other query's answer is computed whole, its duplicates dropped and the
limits bounding it, before its first row is delivered.
A diagnostic the evaluation reports may come between rows delivered as they
are derived.
Once the rows have been delivered the context keeps nothing of the answer,
so answers do not count against
.Fa max_tuples
for later queries, and an answer delivered as it is derived is never held.
The integers and strings a query computes stay interned for the life of the
context, and count against
.Fa max_memory
as every interned value does.
.Pp
.Fn relic_query_collect
copies the answer into a result the caller owns, whole or not at all.
.Fn relic_result_rows
and
.Fn relic_result_columns
give its size, the columns being the query's even when there are no rows,
.Fn relic_result_row
a row's values, a pointer to no values for a query with no columns, or
.Dv NULL
past the last row, and
.Fn relic_result_destroy
releases it, and accepts
.Dv NULL .
A result holds nothing of the context: it may outlive later loads and the
context itself, and holds a copy of the bytes of its symbols and strings,
whose identifiers are its own.
.Fn relic_result_string
reads the bytes of one, as
.Fn relic_string
does for the context's, until the result is destroyed, returning
.Dv NULL
for an identifier the result did not assign, and
.Fn relic_result_compare
orders two of the result's values as
.Fn relic_value_compare
orders the context's, a value whose identifier the result did not assign
comparing equal to everything.
A result's symbol or string is not the context's:
.Fn relic_string
returns
.Dv NULL
for it, and
.Fn relic_value_compare
and
.Fn relic_fact_add
refuse it; an integer is its own value in both.
.Pp
.Fn relic_query_prepare
checks the program first, which must pass, and then
.Fa text ,
which must be exactly one query ending in
.Ql \&? ,
safe as a rule's body must be.
.Fa source_name
names it in diagnostics, is copied, and is
.Qq <query>
when
.Dv NULL .
Preparing adds nothing to the program, so a query refused leaves it as
valid as it was.
.Fn relic_query_count
is the number of queries the program contains, and
.Fn relic_query_loaded
returns the one at
.Fa index ,
counting from zero, or
.Dv NULL
when the program fails its check or holds no such query.
Every query belongs to the caller, and
.Fn relic_query_destroy
releases one and accepts
.Dv NULL .
It may be called before or after
.Fn relic_destroy ,
but a query cannot be run once its context is gone.
.Ss Diagnostics
Every note, warning and error goes to the callback installed with
.Fn relic_diag_set ,
and nowhere else: a context with no callback writes nothing.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
enum relic_diag_level { RELIC_NOTE, RELIC_WARNING, RELIC_ERROR };

struct relic_location {
	const char	*file;
	uint32_t	 line;
	uint32_t	 column;
};

typedef void (*relic_diag_cb)(void *callback_ctx,
    enum relic_diag_level level, const relic_location_t *where,
    const char *message);
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa where
is
.Dv NULL
for a diagnostic that points at no text; a location's line and column count
from one, and its
.Fa file
may be
.Dv NULL .
The message and the location are borrowed for the call, the
.Fa file
name a location holds included, and a callback that keeps either copies
it.
The name lives as long as what was parsed under it: a loaded program's as
long as the context, a prepared query's as long as the query, and an
import's for the call.
A message quotes program text as it was written, so a callback that writes
it to a terminal escapes it first, as
.Fn relic_diag_stderr
does.
A context has no callback until one is installed, and
.Fn relic_diag_set
with
.Dv NULL
removes it.
.Fn relic_diag_stderr
is a ready-made callback that writes each diagnostic to standard error as
.Ql file:line:column: level: message ,
or, when it points at no text, as
.Ql name: level: message ,
where
.Ar name
is the NUL-terminated string its callback context points to, left out with
the colon after it when that is
.Dv NULL .
It writes a backslash as two and any other byte outside printable ASCII as
.Ql \exHH ,
in the file name as in the message.
.Fn relic_error
returns the text of the most recent error, cut to 511 bytes, or
.Dv NULL
when there has been none.
A later success does not clear it, and the string belongs to the context
and is replaced by the next error.
.Pp
.Fn relic_error_class
returns what kind of failure that error was, so that a program can tell
without reading the text, which is for a person and may be worded
differently from one release to the next:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
enum relic_error_class {
	RELIC_ERROR_NONE,
	RELIC_ERROR_REJECTED,
	RELIC_ERROR_EVALUATION,
	RELIC_ERROR_LIMIT,
	RELIC_ERROR_IO,
	RELIC_ERROR_MEMORY,
	RELIC_ERROR_INTERRUPTED,
	RELIC_ERROR_ARGUMENT,
	RELIC_ERROR_INTERNAL
};
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width RELIC_ERROR_INTERRUPTED
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_NONE
there has been no error, and
.Fn relic_error
returns
.Dv NULL ;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_REJECTED
something the language spells was refused, whether in text, a table or a
call: a program or query that does not parse or does not check, a statement
past a structural limit such as 32 arguments, a constant pattern that is not
a regular expression, a table field that is not a value of its column's
type, bytes no symbol is spelled with, a predicate name or number of
arguments no predicate can have;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_EVALUATION
evaluation met a result the language leaves undefined: an integer overflow,
a zero divisor, a string longer than a value can hold, or a pattern computed
during evaluation that is not a regular expression;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_LIMIT
.Fa max_tuples
or
.Fa max_memory
refused the operation, or a capacity of the context was reached, such as
the number of distinct symbols and strings it can hold;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_IO
a file could not be opened, read or closed;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_MEMORY
an allocation failed and no limit was the cause;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_INTERRUPTED
.Fn relic_interrupt
stopped the operation;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_ARGUMENT
the call was malformed in C's terms: a
.Dv NULL
argument, an index, enumerator or flag the library does not know, a value
whose type or identifier is not one the context made, a query of another
context, or a call refused while caller code runs;
.It Dv RELIC_ERROR_INTERNAL
the library found one of its own invariants false, which is a bug in relic;
the text begins
.Ql internal error:\& .
.El
.Pp
The class lasts as the text does: both are set by an error, stand until the
next, and are cleared by no success.
A class added in a later release takes a value after these, so a caller
that switches on the class treats one it does not know as a failure it
cannot classify.
.Pp
.Fn relic_explain_set
installs a callback that receives the plans evaluation runs, one line of
printable ASCII at a time, of at most 1023 bytes and with no newline, a
longer line being cut to end in
.Ql ... :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef void (*relic_explain_cb)(void *callback_ctx, const char *line);
.Ed
.Pp
Each part of the program is shown as it is planned, and one whose
predicates have facts counts them in a line
.Ql facts: N ;
a predicate of facts alone needs no evaluation and is not shown.
A query answered through its rewrite shows the rewrite before its plan.
.Pp
.Fn relic_stats
fills the
.Vt relic_stats_t
.Fa stats
points to, which must not be
.Dv NULL ,
with counters of the work done and of what the context holds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct relic_stats {
	uint64_t	iterations;
	uint64_t	derivations;
	uint64_t	tuples_inserted;
	uint64_t	tuples_scanned;
	uint64_t	tuples_looked_up;
	uint64_t	parallel_rounds;
	size_t		tuples;
	size_t		storage_bytes;
	size_t		symbol_values;
	size_t		string_values;
	size_t		integer_values;
};
.Ed
.Pp
The first six count evaluation's work since the context was created, over
the program's rules and every query's alike:
.Fa iterations
the rounds of the fixed point of recursive components,
.Fa derivations
the head tuples rules produce,
.Fa tuples_inserted
those that added a tuple to its relation, which a row delivered as it is
derived never does, and
.Fa tuples_scanned
and
.Fa tuples_looked_up
the tuples read by scanning a relation and through an index.
The rest describe what it holds when read: the tuples across every
relation, the bytes they and the values occupy, and the distinct symbols,
strings and integers interned.
.Fa parallel_rounds
counts the rounds of evaluation whose work was shared among more than one
thread, and is the one counter that depends on the number of threads.
.Fa derivations
also counts the facts copied into the relation of a predicate rules
derive, not the facts of a predicate no rule derives,
which are read where they are stored.
The rows of each aggregate's body are derived into a relation of their
own, and are counted by
.Fa derivations
and
.Fa tuples_inserted .
.Fa storage_bytes
counts relation and index storage, the table of integers, the symbol pool
and the names of predicates and their sources together, as
.Fa max_memory
does, and
.Fa string_values
and
.Fa integer_values
count the strings and integers evaluation computed as well as those the
program states.
What they count depends on the evaluation strategy and may change from one
release to the next.
.Ss Callbacks
Caller code runs while the context is part way through an operation in four
ways: the diagnostic, explain and row callbacks, and the read function of a
stream given to
.Fn relic_load_file
or
.Fn relic_import_file ,
which may be the caller's own, as
.Xr funopen 3
or
.Xr fopencookie 3
makes one.
Such code may call
.Fn relic_string ,
.Fn relic_error ,
.Fn relic_error_class ,
.Fn relic_value_compare ,
.Fn relic_stats ,
.Fn relic_query_count
and
.Fn relic_interrupt
on its context, and may destroy any query but one being answered.
Any other call on the same context is refused: it returns \-1 or
.Dv NULL ,
or does nothing, and
.Fn relic_error
describes the refusal, and
.Fn relic_error_class
answers
.Dv RELIC_ERROR_ARGUMENT ,
until the next error or until the operation the caller code runs inside
returns.
The diagnostic callback is not called for a refusal, and a refused
.Fn relic_destroy
releases nothing, so a callback that destroys its own context leaks it.
A callback must return: one that leaves by
.Xr longjmp 3
or an exception leaves its context refusing every later call.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions returning
.Vt int
return 0 on success and \-1 on failure, except
.Fn relic_value_compare
and
.Fn relic_result_compare ,
which return a value less than, equal to or greater than zero.
Functions returning a pointer return
.Dv NULL
on failure, and
.Fn relic_result_row ,
.Fn relic_result_string ,
.Fn relic_string
and
.Fn relic_error
also return it when there is no row, identifier or error to return;
.Fn relic_version
never does.
Every failure is explained by an error diagnostic, whose text
.Fn relic_error
and whose class
.Fn relic_error_class
return, except a call refused while caller code runs, which those two
alone describe, and that of
.Fn relic_create
and
.Fn relic_create_config ,
which have no context to report through: they fail only when
.Fa threads
is more than
.Dv RELIC_THREADS_MAX ,
when memory is exhausted, or when
.Xr getentropy 2
fails.
.Fn relic_string ,
.Fn relic_value_compare ,
.Fn relic_query_count ,
.Fn relic_error_class
and
.Fn relic_stats
cannot fail.
.Pp
No function terminates the process on a failure.
A library built for release never calls
.Xr abort 3 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
A complete program that loads a program, answers a query and prints the
first column of each row, reporting any mistake on the standard error.
A symbol is an identifier and is safe to print as it is; a string may hold
any bytes, and a program that prints one escapes it.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#include <relic.h>

static int
print_row(void *ctx, const relic_value_t *values, size_t count)
{
	if (count > 0 && values[0].type == RELIC_SYMBOL)
		puts(relic_string(ctx, values[0].v.id, NULL));

	return (0);
}

int
main(void)
{
	static char name[] = "query";
	relic_query_t *query;
	relic_t *ctx;
	int status;

	ctx = relic_create();
	if (ctx == NULL)
		return (EXIT_FAILURE);
	relic_diag_set(ctx, relic_diag_stderr, name);

	query = NULL;
	status = relic_load_string(ctx, "graph",
	    "edge(a, b). edge(b, c).\en"
	    "path(X, Y) :- edge(X, Y).\en"
	    "path(X, Z) :- edge(X, Y), path(Y, Z).\en");
	if (status == 0)
		query = relic_query_prepare(ctx, NULL, "path(a, X)?");
	if (query == NULL || relic_query_run(ctx, query, print_row, ctx) != 0)
		status = -1;

	relic_query_destroy(query);
	relic_destroy(ctx);

	if (fflush(stdout) == EOF || ferror(stdout))
		status = -1;

	return (status == 0 ? EXIT_SUCCESS : EXIT_FAILURE);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Compiled against the installed library, as
.Dl cc -o query query.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs librelic)
it prints
.Ql b
and
.Ql c ,
each on a line.
.Sh STANDARDS
The header is strict ISO C11 and may be included from C++.
